<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2025-01-23.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="000023"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000023" label="[00:00:23]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; we are simply trying to find the shortest path from hex0 to gcc13</span></div><div class="line" id="000047"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000047" label="[00:00:47]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; kernel can/should be a separate path</span></div><div class="line" id="000049"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000049" label="[00:00:49]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; imo</span></div><div class="line" id="000111"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000111" label="[00:01:11]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, along the way i suspect a fiwix (or linux abi) were necessary, one way or another</span></div><div class="line" id="000144"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000144" label="[00:01:44]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; I have long dropped fiwix</span></div><div class="line" id="000145"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000145" label="[00:01:45]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; now trying to clean up all the other bits I don't actually need or could work around</span></div><div class="line" id="000247"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#000247" label="[00:02:47]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">kernel kinda already is a separate path (KERNEL_BOOTSTRAP flag), but granted things are built that are not required for the non-kernel bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="000311"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000311" label="[00:03:11]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; Yeah like nuking all the python builds saved us a bunch of time</span></div><div class="line" id="000315"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000315" label="[00:03:15]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; looking for any other wins like that</span></div><div class="line" id="000331"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#000331" label="[00:03:31]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yep, gotcha</span></div><div class="line" id="000338"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000338" label="[00:03:38]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; we ask a lot of people to reproduce our bootstrap images, and that currently takes &gt; 1 day</span></div><div class="line" id="000348"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000348" label="[00:03:48]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; so anything we can do to cut that down will encourage more people to reproduce</span></div><div class="line" id="000439"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000439" label="[00:04:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, the assumption then is an existing linux-runtime is out of scope when concerned with arriving at any gcc</span></div><div class="line" id="000512"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#000512" label="[00:05:12]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in terms of speed, trying to build GCC 10 without autogen would be very beneficial (removes autogen and guile from the path)</span></div><div class="line" id="000516"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000516" label="[00:05:16]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; For our use case it is about going from a &quot;from scratch&quot; hex0 container, all the way up to gcc13 x86, and then to a bunch of cross compilers, and then to native packages, which can then replace every alpine/arch/fedora/debian container in the wild and dramatically improve supply chain security.</span></div><div class="line" id="000520"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000520" label="[00:05:20]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It is assumed people are running this from a variety of different container runtimes and kernels</span></div><div class="line" id="000523"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#000523" label="[00:05:23]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">possibly, you don't even require GCC 13</span></div><div class="line" id="000527"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#000527" label="[00:05:27]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and could deal with GCC 10</span></div><div class="line" id="000534"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000534" label="[00:05:34]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">while i was thinking still a _complete_ development host with a clean bootstrapping path towards a linux2.4 with SMP was a desirable baseline for just that, bootstrapping gcc/binutils/gnu based upon that</span></div><div class="line" id="000619"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#000619" label="[00:06:19]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: lrvick's stagex doesn't care about some of the things that live-bootstrap does, such as the kernel bootstrap (which is valid for their case)</span></div><div class="line" id="000641"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#000641" label="[00:06:41]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">they start with a Linux host running some kind of OCI containerisation</span></div><div class="line" id="001030"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001030" label="[00:10:30]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; fossy: actually that is a good point. If we can pivot from x86 gcc10 to x86-x86_64 cross gcc that would save some time</span></div><div class="line" id="001031"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001031" label="[00:10:31]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; provided gcc10 can build cross gcc13</span></div><div class="line" id="001053"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001053" label="[00:10:53]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; but also our goal is to get clang/llvm as early as possible as well and switch to that</span></div><div class="line" id="001209"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#001209" label="[00:12:09]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what's your (i think you call it stage1?) final toolchain?</span></div><div class="line" id="001219"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#001219" label="[00:12:19]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">llvm/musl</span></div><div class="line" id="001221"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#001221" label="[00:12:21]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">?</span></div><div class="line" id="001339"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001339" label="[00:13:39]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; in our model: stage0 is up to M2-Planet, stage1 is up to a modern x86 c compiler, stage2 is cross compilers, and stage3 are native compilers</span></div><div class="line" id="001348"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001348" label="[00:13:48]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; by stage3 we want to be clang/musl</span></div><div class="line" id="001401"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001401" label="[00:14:01]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; if we can drop any steps in the path to that, amazing</span></div><div class="line" id="001418"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#001418" label="[00:14:18]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">okayyy</span></div><div class="line" id="001426"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001426" label="[00:14:26]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; currently stage2 is gcc13 cross compilers built from live-bootstrap x86 gcc 13</span></div><div class="line" id="001507"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#001507" label="[00:15:07]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">can gcc build cross clang?</span></div><div class="line" id="001607"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001607" label="[00:16:07]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; llvm/clang in theory are always cross by design, and we can build modern llvm/clang directly from gcc13</span></div><div class="line" id="001609"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001609" label="[00:16:09]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; but paths earlier than that, I don't know</span></div><div class="line" id="001630"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#001630" label="[00:16:30]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmmmmmmm</span></div><div class="line" id="001642"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001642" label="[00:16:42]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; being able to have a single llvm/clang will be -much- cheaper than having to build gcc13 several times for each arch though</span></div><div class="line" id="001704"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001704" label="[00:17:04]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; so if we could somehow pivot to llvm/clang in stage2 that would be a massive win</span></div><div class="line" id="001713"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#001713" label="[00:17:13]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">has that been attempted yet?</span></div><div class="line" id="001736"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001736" label="[00:17:36]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; nope! currently focused on removing lots of steps that cost us time</span></div><div class="line" id="001738"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001738" label="[00:17:38]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; so we can experiment faster</span></div><div class="line" id="001743"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#001743" label="[00:17:43]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeppp gotcha</span></div><div class="line" id="001914"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001914" label="[00:19:14]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; we are currently trying to multiarch our distro via a native multiarch stage3 with gcc13, then build our actual clang/llvm packages in core, then build the rest of our distro.</span></div><div class="line" id="001934"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001934" label="[00:19:34]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; once that works end to end as a baseline, then we can go make another aggressive pass at bootstrap to try to get clang/llvm as early as possible</span></div><div class="line" id="001938"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001938" label="[00:19:38]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; maybe even in stage1</span></div><div class="line" id="001944"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001944" label="[00:19:44]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; as it would be a massive timesaver</span></div><div class="line" id="013031"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013031" label="[01:30:31]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; What's the benefit of uclibc over musl? Could musl be built with just tcc + a kaem script? If musl could be built that way then in theory it could be moved before bash.</span></div><div class="line" id="013356"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#013356" label="[01:33:56]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">^ this is also feasible i think</span></div><div class="line" id="013437"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013437" label="[01:34:37]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; That would be a much bigger win</span></div><div class="line" id="013448"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013448" label="[01:34:48]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; Also just found this: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/ZilchOS/bootstrap-from-tcc">https://github.com/ZilchOS/bootstrap-from-tcc</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="013510"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013510" label="[01:35:10]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; so this goes from tcc to busybox</span></div><div class="line" id="013538"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013538" label="[01:35:38]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; then to gcc4 gcc10 and then clang</span></div><div class="line" id="013934"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013934" label="[01:39:34]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; he goes from tinycc to musl to busybox with a single c file run by tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="013939"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013939" label="[01:39:39]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/ZilchOS/bootstrap-from-tcc/blob/main/recipes/1-stage1.c">https://github.com/ZilchOS/bootstrap-from-tcc/blob/main/recipes/1-stage1.c</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="013947"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013947" label="[01:39:47]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; I can't tell if this is insane or amazing. both?</span></div><div class="line" id="014328"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014328" label="[01:43:28]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; So in his case he has no libc, so he starts with -just- tinycc as his seed and basically shoehorns in the basic libc functions he needs into this one c file</span></div><div class="line" id="014338"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014338" label="[01:43:38]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah</span></div><div class="line" id="014345"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014345" label="[01:43:45]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; wild</span></div><div class="line" id="014354"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014354" label="[01:43:54]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">both insane and amazing, yes haha</span></div><div class="line" id="014411"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014411" label="[01:44:11]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">x86_64 as well, hm</span></div><div class="line" id="014432"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014432" label="[01:44:32]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; but since we actually have M2Libc by this point, this could be cleaned up and made much easier to read</span></div><div class="line" id="014440"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014440" label="[01:44:40]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">bits from here could absolutely be lifted to do musl earlier</span></div><div class="line" id="014449"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014449" label="[01:44:49]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; well, by this point we have mes libc</span></div><div class="line" id="014459"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014459" label="[01:44:59]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; yeah that is what I am thinking</span></div><div class="line" id="014513"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014513" label="[01:45:13]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i wouldn't keep it in C, because we have no reason to, but that might be cool</span></div><div class="line" id="014525"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014525" label="[01:45:25]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmmmmmm</span></div><div class="line" id="014530"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014530" label="[01:45:30]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">thanks for posting this</span></div><div class="line" id="014530"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014530" label="[01:45:30]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; open source everything. It will help randos you don't know years from now with problems you never thought about</span></div><div class="line" id="014610"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014610" label="[01:46:10]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; but yeah being able to pivot from mes to musl/busybox right away would be a major unlock I think</span></div><div class="line" id="014629"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014629" label="[01:46:29]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; I don't think we need to keep any of this code, but someone working out the steps saves a lot of time</span></div><div class="line" id="014715"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014715" label="[01:47:15]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, a lot of it past that first musl/busybox bit is of limited utility (imo), as they don't care about pregened files + it's actually roughly the same minus utils</span></div><div class="line" id="014750"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014750" label="[01:47:50]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">however it would make the start wayyy cleaner</span></div><div class="line" id="014753"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014753" label="[01:47:53]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which i am 100% for</span></div><div class="line" id="014819"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014819" label="[01:48:19]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; Exactly. Can throw a ton of awkward early steps out he window</span></div><div class="line" id="014913"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014913" label="[01:49:13]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; I am glad my rabbit hole researching prior art pays off every once in a while. ha ha</span></div><div class="line" id="014957"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014957" label="[01:49:57]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the web of dependencies is complicated. there is always bits and pieces that are really helpful :D</span></div><div class="line" id="015007"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#015007" label="[01:50:07]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and you very well may have found one</span></div><div class="line" id="015043"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#015043" label="[01:50:43]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; bootstrapping is a fun archeology game</span></div><div class="line" id="015210"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#015210" label="[01:52:10]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; Red Hat employee. Huh.</span></div><div class="line" id="015220"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#015220" label="[01:52:20]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; Should try to lure him over here</span></div><div class="line" id="020838"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#020838" label="[02:08:38]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: what's wrong with our early steps though?</span></div><div class="line" id="020908"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#020908" label="[02:09:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">between tcc and musl there aren't that many steps anyway and they are fairly fast</span></div><div class="line" id="021053"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#021053" label="[02:10:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we had to write some custom Makefiles there but that actually helps a lot on other arches</span></div><div class="line" id="021214"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#021214" label="[02:12:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, it's mostly because of tcc that we go there fast</span></div><div class="line" id="021244"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#021244" label="[02:12:44]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">live-bootstrap goes slowly during mes stage, then is fairly fast and gets slower once we reach GCC4</span></div><div class="line" id="023020"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023020" label="[02:30:20]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: nothing &quot;wrong&quot; imo, only that it would be cleaner i think</span></div><div class="line" id="023025"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023025" label="[02:30:25]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, no benefits from a speed standpoint</span></div><div class="line" id="023044"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023044" label="[02:30:44]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">s/no/minimal</span></div><div class="line" id="023552"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023552" label="[02:35:52]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we could (probably) replace patch, gzip, tar, sed, bzip2, coreutils, bash, grep, diffutils, which and gawk; and maybe curl &amp; dhcpcd</span></div><div class="line" id="023559"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023559" label="[02:35:59]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for at least some portion of the bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="023613"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#023613" label="[02:36:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, gzip tar and bzip2 are now optional</span></div><div class="line" id="023620"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#023620" label="[02:36:20]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since mescc-tools-extra can unpack sources</span></div><div class="line" id="023642"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023642" label="[02:36:42]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mm, true</span></div><div class="line" id="023646"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023646" label="[02:36:46]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">would be interesting to see how long it can last</span></div><div class="line" id="023711"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023711" label="[02:37:11]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but you make a good point that i don't think lrvick will see much speed increases if this was implemented</span></div><div class="line" id="023736"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#023736" label="[02:37:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though if you want them to last longer, perhaps rebuilding mescc-tools-extra with tcc would be needed</span></div><div class="line" id="024048"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#024048" label="[02:40:48]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">GCC cross-compilation can eventually be eliminated by porting live-bootstrap to x86_64...</span></div><div class="line" id="024108"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#024108" label="[02:41:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but at the moment tcc-mes is buggy and is not able to fully build meslibc</span></div><div class="line" id="024114"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#024114" label="[02:41:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(it can build some of the files)</span></div><div class="line" id="024132"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#024132" label="[02:41:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and I don't think there was any progress there for about a year or so</span></div><div class="line" id="024248"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#024248" label="[02:42:48]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mes-&gt;tcc is a very awkward step :(</span></div><div class="line" id="024918"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#024918" label="[02:49:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, it's scheme</span></div><div class="line" id="024953"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#024953" label="[02:49:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we should at some point try to update nyacc...</span></div><div class="line" id="025006"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#025006" label="[02:50:06]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but right now we use Googulators fork with patches to regenerate some files</span></div><div class="line" id="025551"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025551" label="[02:55:51]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; &gt; between tcc and musl there aren't that many steps anyway and they are fairly fast</span></div><div class="line" id="025551"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025551" label="[02:55:51]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I am actually optimizing for two things</span></div><div class="line" id="025551"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025551" label="[02:55:51]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">1. minimal code, as ultimately for some of my use cases I am going to have to budget/coordinate an audit of everything in the bootstrap chain. Every step we skip, even if fast, is less code malware can hide in</span></div><div class="line" id="025554"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025554" label="[02:55:54]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">2. build speed, to justify as many people as possible being able to reproduce on a very diverse range of (even low spec) hardware</span></div><div class="line" id="025617"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#025617" label="[02:56:17]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in the (idealistic, maybe infeasible) long term, i would love to do M2-Planet-&gt;(something that doesn't exist yet)-&gt;gcc 4</span></div><div class="line" id="025649"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#025649" label="[02:56:49]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and skip mes + tinycc entirely</span></div><div class="line" id="025755"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#025755" label="[02:57:55]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: yeah, the diff doesn't look huge for Googulator's fork if i am observing this correctly?</span></div><div class="line" id="025800"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#025800" label="[02:58:00]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">should be somewhat straightforward?</span></div><div class="line" id="025926"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#025926" label="[02:59:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, I think so</span></div><div class="line" id="025936"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#025936" label="[02:59:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, ideally it's upstreamed</span></div><div class="line" id="025945"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#025945" label="[02:59:45]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but perhaps we need to test it with newer nyacc first</span></div><div class="line" id="030000"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#030000" label="[03:00:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mes should support much newer versions these days</span></div><div class="line" id="030041"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#030041" label="[03:00:41]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, upstreaming would be good</span></div><div class="line" id="030107"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#030107" label="[03:01:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, M2-Planet is also getting better</span></div><div class="line" id="030123"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#030123" label="[03:01:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it might be not that far behind mescc...</span></div><div class="line" id="030145"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#030145" label="[03:01:45]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's jus that mescc was debugged specifically to build tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="030337"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#030337" label="[03:03:37]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> mmmm</span></div><div class="line" id="030443"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#030443" label="[03:04:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tinycc is not fully C compatible in a sense that e.g. it depends on int being 32-bit</span></div><div class="line" id="030452"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#030452" label="[03:04:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">whereas spec does not guaranteed that</span></div><div class="line" id="085019"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#085019" label="[08:50:19]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator91 rekado time to celebrate: microhs is bootstrappable with hugs <a rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.guix.gnu.org/75778">https://issues.guix.gnu.org/75778</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="090322"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#090322" label="[09:03:22]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so much better than nhc98</span></div><div class="line" id="134514"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#134514" label="[13:45:14]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; @irc_libera_homo:stikonas.eu: nice</span></div><div class="line" id="140659"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#140659" label="[14:06:59]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;build speed, to justify as many people as possible being able to reproduce on a very diverse range of (even low spec) hardware&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="140816"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#140816" label="[14:08:16]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as soon as c++ enters the scene with g++ and/or llvm/clang needed it's a lost cause</span></div><div class="line" id="140909"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#140909" label="[14:09:09]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">however, tinycc support can be extended far beyond a minimal package set towards a one for a complete development host (with linux2.4 that is here)</span></div><div class="line" id="140948"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#140948" label="[14:09:48]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, i just got molested with extortion letters from bureaucracy here, chances are i'll have to terminate my work, _again_</span></div><div class="line" id="141148"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#141148" label="[14:11:48]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, another principle issue, bootstrapping of bootloader/kernel and assembler needed shouldn't be excluded with any approach</span></div><div class="line" id="141351"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#141351" label="[14:13:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as soon as you transition beyond linux2.4; avoiding gcc/binutils or llvm/clang early during bootstrapping becomes very complicated then</span></div><div class="line" id="141741"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#141741" label="[14:17:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the kexec towards fiwix is an excellent solution, and currently the only one that does work; except for the fact it introduces an additional kernel needed, regardless of how minimal that kernel was</span></div><div class="line" id="142533"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#142533" label="[14:25:33]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">from a practical standpoint, another distinction was exactly when a sufficiently complete development host is available that can be booted on some real hardware with sufficient hardware support</span></div><div class="line" id="142815"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#142815" label="[14:28:15]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">currently, that depends on some linux-4.x and with it several dozens of millions of lines of code for toolchain and kernel, all of which can be avoided being introduced too early in the bootstrapping chain to arrive at a bootable and sufficiently complete development host</span></div><div class="line" id="143946"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#143946" label="[14:39:46]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in theory, the kexec towards fiwix could be replaced with kexec towards linux-2.4 instantly, in practice there is no benefit in particular when fiwix cannot aim for a complete development host with sufficient hardware support, but is utilized as an intermediate bootstrapping dependency only</span></div><div class="line" id="144042"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#144042" label="[14:40:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fiwix is the better option for the use case of an intermediate dependency, linux-2.4 is a better option for sufficient hardware support</span></div><div class="line" id="144230"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#144230" label="[14:42:30]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">however, i would argue, a bootable linux-2.4/tinycc initial development host is a preferrable option over linux-4/gcc</span></div><div class="line" id="144355"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#144355" label="[14:43:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although this would extend the bootstrappable dependency chain itself (depending on when the job is considered done), it would greatly reduce the amount of lines of code and dependencies for a complete development host</span></div><div class="line" id="153036"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#153036" label="[15:30:36]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://debbugs.gnu.org/cgi/bugreport.cgi?bug=75778">https://debbugs.gnu.org/cgi/bugreport.cgi?bug=75778</a>  that's indeed great news:</span></div><div class="line" id="153048"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#153048" label="[15:30:48]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although ;; TODO: CONF=unix-32 if CPU is 32-bit.</span></div><div class="line" id="153108"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#153108" label="[15:31:08]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">We will need that to continue the bootstrap towards GHC, since early GHC is 32-bit only.</span></div><div class="line" id="153218"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#153218" label="[15:32:18]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">GHC 4.08.x seems to be a viable target to build using mhs - 4.08 and 4.08.1 mention &quot;GHC &gt; 2.10 required&quot;, 4.08.2 says &quot;GHC required, preferably 4.08x&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="153250"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#153250" label="[15:32:50]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which sounds like it's Haskell 1.4 / Haskell98 code (with who-knows-what extensions, sadly)</span></div><div class="line" id="153438"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#153438" label="[15:34:38]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">best part is, 4.08.2 is where Guix's bootstrap chain currently starts, and with my WIP work, that can propagate all the way to 9.x for x86-32</span></div><div class="line" id="153447"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#153447" label="[15:34:47]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(cross-compiling to x86-64 is TODO)</span></div><div class="line" id="153620"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#153620" label="[15:36:20]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if we can do MicroHs -&gt; GHC 4.08.2, that completes the chain from a basic C/C++ development environment without anything functional or lazy all the way up to modern GHC</span></div><div class="line" id="172709"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#172709" label="[17:27:09]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I guess it counts as bootstrap of cabal <a rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.guix.gnu.org/75787">https://issues.guix.gnu.org/75787</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="225106"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225106" label="[22:51:06]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: what's the status of 4.08.x -&gt; 9.x for GHC?</span></div><div class="line" id="225315"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#225315" label="[22:53:15]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">presumably just finding a path there</span></div><div class="line" id="225332"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#225332" label="[22:53:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it should be much easier once you have GHC since that's what GHC people used for development</span></div><br /></div></body></html>